<details class="group border border-surface-container-high rounded-xl bg-surface-container-lowest p-6 cursor-pointer open:bg-surface-container-low transition-colors shadow-sm"><summary class="font-headline-sm text-primary text-[18px] flex justify-between items-center list-none font-semibold">Can jaw disease cause facial swelling?<span class="material-symbols-outlined group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform text-secondary">expand_more</span></summary><p class="mt-4 font-body-md text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">Yes — infections, cysts, and tumours all cause swelling. New or growing swelling should always be evaluated.</p></details>
<details class="group border border-surface-container-high rounded-xl bg-surface-container-lowest p-6 cursor-pointer open:bg-surface-container-low transition-colors shadow-sm"><summary class="font-headline-sm text-primary text-[18px] flex justify-between items-center list-none font-semibold">What imaging is best for jaw disease?<span class="material-symbols-outlined group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform text-secondary">expand_more</span></summary><p class="mt-4 font-body-md text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">An OPG is a good first step. A CBCT (3D scan) gives much more detail. MRI is best for soft tissue and joints.</p></details>
<details class="group border border-surface-container-high rounded-xl bg-surface-container-lowest p-6 cursor-pointer open:bg-surface-container-low transition-colors shadow-sm"><summary class="font-headline-sm text-primary text-[18px] flex justify-between items-center list-none font-semibold">Is jaw disease hereditary?<span class="material-symbols-outlined group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform text-secondary">expand_more</span></summary><p class="mt-4 font-body-md text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">Some forms are — ameloblastoma, certain cysts, and some arthritic conditions. Family history is relevant.</p></details>
<details class="group border border-surface-container-high rounded-xl bg-surface-container-lowest p-6 cursor-pointer open:bg-surface-container-low transition-colors shadow-sm"><summary class="font-headline-sm text-primary text-[18px] flex justify-between items-center list-none font-semibold">Can jaw disease affect my teeth?<span class="material-symbols-outlined group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform text-secondary">expand_more</span></summary><p class="mt-4 font-body-md text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">Yes. Cysts and tumours can displace or loosen teeth. Infections can spread to teeth and vice versa.</p></details>
